An original game. No names, characters, sprites, models, sounds or code
from any other game or franchise. Nothing from PokéSurvivor (its code and
sprites are non-commercial) or SpriteCollab was used or consulted for code.

Art and sound
- Models come from the per-style asset library
  (@netnetcapital/asset-library): CC0 1.0 models by Quaternius (wickbearers,
  the dead) and Kenney (columns, crates and rocks), each pack listed
  with its source URL and licence verification in that package's NOTICE and
  manifest/packs.json. The files are fetched and built, never committed
  here; the build copies only the models this game uses.
- Fallback shapes (client/library.ts) are generated from three.js
  primitives; textures are drawn on a canvas at load.

Dependencies (unmodified, via npm)
- three.js 0.186.1 (https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js), MIT.
- nipplejs 1.0.4 (https://github.com/yoannmoinet/nipplejs), MIT.
- @netnetcapital/engine and @netnetcapital/engine-sim (this repository);
  see their NOTICE files (bitECS MPL-2.0, Rapier Apache-2.0, Colyseus MIT,
  zod MIT).

Patterns (ideas only)
- The dungeon-crawl and survivor-like genres' general mechanics (rooms
  joined by corridors, rooms that wake as you near them, loot in cleared
  rooms, permadeath runs, auto-firing weapons, pick-one-of-three
  level-ups, a boss in the deepest room). Mechanics, not expression; no
  level, item, enemy or text was copied from any game. The floor plan is
  generated by our own code (engine dungeon.ts) from a seed.
- Flow-field pathing (a breadth-first distance field steered down its
  gradient), a textbook technique, implemented in engine-sim walls.ts.
- Hit-stop, screen shake and damage numbers as described in common
  game-feel talks and articles (e.g. "Juice it or lose it", GDC 2012).
